{"name":"Travertine Path","hex":"#b5ab8f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"travertine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light, porous form of concretionary limestone (or calcite) deposited from solution, and sometimes quarried for building."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/travertine"},{"term":"path","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A trail for the use of, or worn by, pedestrians.","A course taken."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a path in, or on (something), or for (someone).","To navigate through a file system directory tree (to a desired file or folder)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/path"}],"entries":[{"title":"Travertine","blocks":[{"text":"Travertine is a form of fresh water limestone deposited around mineral springs, especially hot springs. It often has a fibrous or concentric appearance and exists in white, tan, cream-colored, and rusty varieties. It is formed by a process of rapid precipitation of calcium carbonate, often at the mouth of a hot spring or in a limestone cave. In the latter, it can form stalactites, stalagmites, and other speleothems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Mammoth_Terraces.jpg/330px-Mammoth_Terraces.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Travertine"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Kalktuff-Mosscrusts.jpg/500px-Kalktuff-Mosscrusts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Kalktuff-Mosscrusts.jpg/1280px-Kalktuff-Mosscrusts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Kalktuff-Mosscrusts.jpg/500px-Kalktuff-Mosscrusts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"